insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('17c4c656-d34b-54bd-8e0b-64e9e073187d'::uuid,'57e7eec1-e655-50fd-8bc4-e7b956b654fc'::uuid,'Consolidated Draft 1.0',E'# Chapter 9. Public Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development\n\n| **Chapter at a Glance** |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n|-------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| What this chapter does  | Builds capacity outside the office: the City DRRM training plan, the exercise programme, public awareness with reach targets, risk communication, BDRRMC support, volunteers, evacuation centre standards and inclusive consultation. |\n| Main procedures         | SOP 9-01 to 9-09                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| Read with               | Chapters 2, 4 and 8                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| Status                  | Draft 1.0, 12 November 2026. Working draft for validation (workshop 1–4 December 2026); not approved.                                                                                                                                 |\n\n## 1. Introduction and Assessment-Informed Rationale\n\nA warning works only if people understand it and know what to do. A contingency plan works only if the people named in it have practised it. A barangay can account for its residents within hours only if its BDRRMC is organised, trained and has done it before. This chapter is about those people: the public, barangay officials and volunteers, school and hospital staff, City office personnel and the EOC roster. It sets how the City builds their awareness and their skills, and how it keeps those skills from fading.\n\nChapter 2 develops the CDRRMO''s own staff (competency framework, training needs analysis and SOP HR-04). Chapter 4 issues warnings and notices during events. This chapter covers capacity beyond the CDRRMO and between events: public awareness, the City''s annual DRRM training and exercise programme, barangay and community resilience, volunteers, and the readiness of schools, hospitals and evacuation centres.\n\nThe baseline shows activity without measurement (Figure 9.1). Domain A7 (Preparedness and Community Capacity) has an index of **2.12 (Developing)**, with 2 of 5 Mandatory items compliant. Training, drills and IEC happen, but most scores rest on the FGD alone and fell to 2 under the evidence rule: the training calendar, drill evaluations, IEC reach and BDRRMC functionality are all still to be shown. Evacuation centres do not yet meet the Ligtas Pinoy Centers standards (A7.4, High). There is no refresher cycle (A7.8), no skills inventory across offices and barangays (A2.10), and no record of a barangay accounting for its residents within a set time (A7.9) (Table 9.1).\n\n**Figure 9.1**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Scores for Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Indicators*\n\n![Figure 96: IAT v1.7 Scores for Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Indicators](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-96.webp)\n\n*Note. Scores copied from the A_CDRRMO and B_EOC sheets of IAT v1.7. The A7 domain index is Σ(score × weight) ÷ Σweight over A7.1 to A7.9 = 36 ÷ 17 = 2.12, banded on the unrounded value. A7.5 (prepositioned stocks, score 3) is owned by Chapter 6 and not shown. All rows are pending validation at the workshop of 1–4 December 2026.*\n\n**Table 9.1**\n\n*Assessment Findings That Shape This Chapter*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (IAT v1.7)**                                                                             | **Score** | **Priority** | **Finding (Summarised From the Workbook)**                                                                                                                                        |\n|--------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A7.4   | Permanent evacuation centres planned or established; existing centres meet minimum standards (M, w3) | 2         | High (9.0)   | ECs do not yet meet RA 12076 standards; the 10-Year Plan plans retrofits of 30 ECs and MOAs for private alternate ECs. No standards check was done during the October site visit. |\n| A7.1   | Annual training programme for City personnel, BDRRMCs, schools and communities (M, w2)               | 2         | Medium (6.0) | FGD self-score of 3 lowered to 2; training calendar and attendance to be validated.                                                                                               |\n| A7.2   | Quarterly NSED and hazard-specific drills with evaluation (M, w2)                                    | 2         | Medium (6.0) | FGD self-score of 4 lowered to 2; drill evaluation reports to be validated.                                                                                                       |\n| B7.3   | Annual exercise programme testing EOC activation (M, w2)                                             | 2         | Medium (6.0) | EOC activation not tested on its own; add an annual functional exercise.                                                                                                          |\n| B7.2   | EOC staff trained (EOC operations, ICS, information management) with records (M, w2)                 | 2         | Medium (6.0) | ICS all levels, EOC management and MDM courses still needed.                                                                                                                      |\n| A7.9   | BDRRMCs account for residents of affected puroks within 12 hours, shown in a drill or event (GP, w2) | 1         | Medium (6.0) | No drill or AAR record of purok welfare checks; headcounts are informal.                                                                                                          |\n| A7.3   | BDRRMC functionality across 85 barangays assessed, with a support plan (GP, w2)                      | 2         | Medium (4.0) | FGD self-score of 3 lowered to 2; functionality matrix to be validated.                                                                                                           |\n| A6.4   | Warnings in the local language and accessible formats (GP, w2)                                       | 2         | Medium (4.0) | FGD self-score of 4 lowered to 2; sample advisories in Ormocanon (Bisaya) and accessible formats to be validated.                                                                            |\n| A2.10  | Skills inventory across CDRRMO, partner offices and barangays, updated within 12 months (GP, w1)     | 1         | Low (3.0)    | No skills inventory.                                                                                                                                                              |\n| A7.8   | Annual refresher cycle for responders, EOC staff and BDRRMC members (GP, w1)                         | 1         | Low (3.0)    | No refresher cycle set.                                                                                                                                                           |\n| A7.6   | Inclusive DRRM, with sector representatives confirming engagement (M, w2)                            | 3         | Low (3.0)    | IPGDO on the CDRRMC; inclusive EWS, IEC and children''s programmes planned in the CDP; sector confirmation not yet gathered.                                                       |\n| A6.5   | Public information officer and risk communication plan (GP, w1)                                      | 2         | Low (2.0)    | No approved risk communication plan; planned in the 10-Year Plan and the CDP.                                                                                                     |\n| A6.6   | IEC programme on hazards and preparedness with annual reach targets (GP, w1)                         | 2         | Low (2.0)    | FGD self-score of 3 lowered to 2; reach targets to be validated.                                                                                                                  |\n| A2.8   | Volunteer management system, including ACDV mobilisation for anticipatory action (GP, w1)            | 3         | Low (1.0)    | Accreditation and a partner MOA exist and are used; no registry, deployment or insurance rules; MOA copy unsigned.                                                                |\n| A7.7   | Schools, hospitals and critical facilities included in preparedness planning and drills (GP, w1)     | 3         | Low (1.0)    | Tertiary-institutions MOA and school coordinator training exist; joint drills to be validated.                                                                                    |\n\n*Note. Priority is the workbook band of (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 (Mandatory) or × 1.0 (Good Practice): High 9 or more, Medium 4 to 8.9, Low below 4. A2.8 and B7.2 are shared with Chapter 2; A6.4 and A6.5 with Chapter 4. Annex 9-A gives the full row set.*\n\n### 1.1 What the Records Show\n\n- **Training mandates and plans.** CDRRMC Res. No. 09, s. 2024 adopts the Incident Command System and directs the CDRRMO to train City and barangay officials and responders in it. The CDP 2024–2030 programmes standard training modules (PPA 1.1.6), capacity building for CDRRMC agencies and BDRRMCs, a local pool of trainers and simulation exercises (PPAs 2.6.1 to 2.6.3), school DRRM coordinator training (PPA 2.16) and the ACDV roster, insurance and training (PPAs 2.7.1 to 2.7.3). The 10-Year Plan lists ICS at all levels, EOC management, MDM, CCCM, RDANA and PDNA training. No training needs analysis covering offices and barangays was found.\n\n- **Community health and volunteers.** Ordinance No. 171, s. 2024 institutionalises Barangay Health Emergency Response Teams in all barangays. Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024 institutionalises the City volunteer programme under RA 9418. CDRRMC Res. No. 07, s. 2024 approves the accreditation of community disaster volunteers; Res. Nos. 03 and 04, s. 2022 cover their guidelines and insurance. The CPR-RIMT Rapid Rescue Riders augment HEMS and the CDRRMO under SP Res. No. 2023-347.\n\n- **Barangay plans.** EO 2023-0085 created the Barangay DRRM Plan Review Team, led by the CDRRMO Head with the CPDO, using the Enhanced Quality Assurance System of NDRRMC MC No. 03, s. 2022 (Chapter 3). Certificates of review were not in the document set.\n\n- **Evacuation centres.** The 10-Year Plan plans retrofits of 30 evacuation centres, mostly open covered courts, and MOAs for private alternate centres. The CDRA rates evacuation centres and covered courts "Poor" for vulnerability. The IAT recommends auditing centres against RA 12076 and DSWD standards and prioritising the 46 critical flood barangays.\n\n- **Inclusion.** The IPGDO sits on the CDRRMC. CDP PPAs 1.2.6 (gender-sensitive and inclusive EWS SOP), 2.1 (inclusive IEC and risk communication) and 2.20 (Comprehensive Emergency Program for Children) are planned.\n\n- **Schools.** CDRRMC Res. No. 04, s. 2025 creates an Education Resilience and DRRM Coordination Group with all tertiary institutions; DepEd SDO, the Red Cross and DOH-CHD HEMS sit on the CDRRMC; Ordinance Nos. 135, s. 2023 and 189, s. 2025 set rules on suspending classes and work.\n\n### 1.2 Design Considerations\n\n1.  **Competence, not attendance.** A training counts when the person can do the task, shown in an exercise or real event. Attendance sheets are records, not proof.\n\n2.  **Capacity drains.** Staff move, barangay officials change after each election, and unused skills fade. Every capacity has a refresher date (Figure 9.2).\n\n3.  **Practise in order.** Drills, then tabletop exercises, then functional and full-scale exercises; each evaluated, each finding tracked.\n\n4.  **Barangay first.** The first responders in Ormoc are its 85 BDRRMCs and their puroks. Support goes first to the barangays that are least functional and most exposed.\n\n5.  **Reach, not activity.** IEC is measured by the people and households reached and what they know or do, not by the number of seminars held.\n\n6.  **Inclusive by design.** Messages, trainings, drills and evacuation centres are designed with women, children, older persons, persons with disabilities and other at-risk groups, not adjusted afterwards.\n\n## 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter sets how the City builds and sustains awareness and capacity for disaster risk reduction and management across its people, communities and institutions.\n\nIntended results:\n\n1.  An annual City DRRM training programme, based on a needs analysis of City offices, BDRRMCs, schools, responders and volunteers, is approved and delivered, with evidence of competence (A7.1).\n\n2.  Every trained role has a refresher date, and attendance and lapse are tracked (A7.8); a skills inventory across offices and barangays is updated every 12 months (A2.10).\n\n3.  An annual exercise programme, from NSED drills to an EOC functional exercise and a full-scale exercise, is run and evaluated (A7.2, B7.3).\n\n4.  An IEC programme with annual reach targets per barangay is delivered in Ormocanon (Bisaya) and accessible formats (A6.4, A6.6), under an approved risk communication plan (A6.5).\n\n5.  All 85 BDRRMCs are assessed for functionality each year, low-functioning barangays receive a support package (A7.3), and each barangay can account for residents of affected puroks within 12 hours (A7.9).\n\n6.  The volunteer programme has a registry, accreditation, insurance, training and deployment rules (A2.8).\n\n7.  Evacuation centres are audited against national standards and improved in priority order (A7.4); schools and hospitals take part in planning and drills (A7.7).\n\n8.  Sector representatives confirm that they were engaged in plans, trainings and drills (A7.6).\n\n## 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\n**In scope:** public awareness and IEC between events; the risk communication plan; the City DRRM training programme for City offices, BDRRMCs, BHERTs, schools, responders, volunteers and the EOC roster; refresher cycles; the skills inventory; the exercise programme; BDRRMC functionality and support; barangay welfare checks; the volunteer programme; evacuation centre standards; preparedness of schools and hospitals; inclusive DRRM.\n\n**Table 9.2**\n\n*Subjects Handled in Other Chapters*\n\n| **Subject**                                                                                                          | **Where It Is Handled**             |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| CDRRMO staff competency framework, TNA and learning and development (HR-04); HR rules for volunteers and surge staff | Chapter 2 (sections 12, 14.3, 15.3) |\n| Barangay DRRM plan review and certificates of review                                                                 | Chapter 3 (A3.7; EO 2023-0085)      |\n| Issuing warnings, public notices of a State of Imminent Disaster and event-time public information                   | Chapter 4 (A6.3, A6.7)              |\n| Evacuation operations and camp management                                                                            | Chapter 4; CCCM Team (Chapter 8)    |\n| Structural assessment of evacuation centres by the Building Official                                                 | Chapter 8 (Part C, C2.1)            |\n| Funding of training, exercises and EC retrofits                                                                      | Chapter 6 (LDRRMFIP)                |\n| Evaluation of exercises and AARs; data protection                                                                    | Chapter 7 (SOPs 7-04, 7-09)         |\n| Partners in trainings and exercises; mutual aid exercises                                                            | Chapter 8                           |\n\n**Institutional boundaries.** The CDRRMO plans, coordinates and delivers DRRM training and exercises and keeps the records. The City HRMO keeps personnel training records for City employees. Barangays decide on their own BDRRMC composition and activities within the law; the City supports, it does not direct. DepEd and the hospitals own their internal preparedness; the CDRRMO plans and drills with them. Volunteers work under the rules of the City volunteer programme and the tasking of the EOC or the Incident Commander.\n\n## 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\n**Table 9.3**\n\n*Instruments Governing Awareness, Capacity Development and Community Resilience*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                                             | **What It Requires or Allows**                                                                                                                                             | **How This Chapter Applies It** |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)                                                                                        | LDRRMO functions, including organising training, orientation and knowledge management activities and raising public awareness (items cited by IAT v1.7 for A6.6 and A7.1). | SOPs 9-01 to 9-04               |\n| RA 10121 Sec. 13                                                                                           | Accreditation, mobilisation and protection of disaster volunteers.                                                                                                         | SOP 9-07                        |\n| RA 10121 Sec. 14                                                                                           | Integration of DRR education into school curricula and the Sangguniang Kabataan programme; mandatory DRR training for public sector employees.                             | SOPs 9-01, 9-02; section 11     |\n| NDRRMC Nationwide Simultaneous Earthquake Drill guidelines                                                 | Quarterly participation in the NSED.                                                                                                                                       | SOP 9-03                        |\n| NDRRMC MC No. 4, s. 2012 (ICS); CDRRMC Res. No. 09, s. 2024                                                | ICS as the standard; training of City and barangay officials and responders.                                                                                               | SOP 9-02                        |\n| DILG MC No. 2025-035 (Listo si KAP)                                                                        | Assessment of barangay preparedness (as cited in IAT v1.7 for A7.3).                                                                                                       | SOP 9-06                        |\n| RA 9418 (Volunteer Act of 2007); Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024                                                | Local volunteer programme.                                                                                                                                                 | SOP 9-07                        |\n| Ordinance No. 171, s. 2024                                                                                 | BHERTs in all barangays.                                                                                                                                                   | Section 9.3; SOP 9-06           |\n| RA 12076 (Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act); DSWD standards; NDRRMC Disaster Preparedness Minimum Standards Vol. 2 | Permanent, resilient evacuation centres and minimum standards for their facilities and services.                                                                           | SOP 9-08; Annex 9-D             |\n| RA 10821 (Children''s Emergency Relief and Protection Act)                                                  | Protection of children in emergencies, including limits on the use of schools as evacuation centres.                                                                       | Section 11                      |\n| RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 9994; RA 11106 (Filipino Sign Language Act); RA 8371                       | Rights and access of women, persons with disabilities, older persons, Deaf persons and indigenous peoples.                                                                 | Section 11.3; SOPs 9-04, 9-05   |\n| RA 10639 (Free Mobile Disaster Alerts Act)                                                                 | Mobile alerts as one channel among several.                                                                                                                                | Section 6                       |\n| RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Secs. 1(a), 2 and 5                                                                   | Inclusion in anticipatory action; public notice; mobilisation of accredited volunteers for anticipatory action.                                                            | Sections 6.4 and 10             |\n\n*Note. RA 10121 Sec. 12(c) item numbers follow IAT v1.7 and are to be checked against the official text. The official text governs.*\n\n## 5. Systems Thinking: Capacity as a Stock\n\nCapacity behaves like water in a tank with a leak (Figure 9.2). Training fills it; staff turnover, barangay elections and unused skills drain it. A City that trains intensively once and then stops will see its capacity fall back within a few years, however good the training was. Two levers matter more than the size of any single course: a refresher cycle and regular exercises, which slow the drain, and a skills inventory, which shows how full the tank is.\n\n**Figure 9.2**\n\n*Capacity as a Stock With Inflows and Outflows*\n\n![Figure 97: Capacity as a Stock With Inflows and Outflows](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-97.webp)\n\n*Note. Prepared for this chapter after Meadows (2008). Each barangay election is a planned outflow that the training calendar should anticipate.*\n\n- **Plan for the outflow.** After each barangay election, the CDRRMO runs an orientation for new BDRRMC members early in their term.\n\n- **Trainers multiply capacity.** A local pool of trainers (CDP PPA 2.6.2), drawn from the CDRRMO, BHERTs, volunteers and partners, lets the City reach 85 barangays without waiting for outside courses.\n\n- **Exercises are where capacity is proven.** Each evaluated exercise is evidence for the IAT at levels 3 and 4.\n\n## 6. Public Awareness and Risk Communication\n\n### 6.1 The IEC Programme\n\nThe IEC programme sets, for each year, the messages, audiences, channels and reach targets per barangay (A6.6). Messages follow the hazards and plans of Chapter 3: what each warning means, where the evacuation centre is, what to bring, how to check on neighbours, and what a State of Imminent Disaster is and what anticipatory actions it may bring. Reach is counted as people or households reached, by barangay, sex and age group where possible, and a short knowledge check is used in a sample of barangays each year.\n\n**Table 9.4**\n\n*IEC Channels and Their Use*\n\n| **Channel**                                             | **Use**                                                   | **Reach Measure**                                        |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|\n| Barangay assemblies and purok meetings                  | Hazard maps, evacuation routes, family plans              | Attendance by barangay and purok                         |\n| Schools (with DepEd SDO and tertiary institutions)      | Learner and family preparedness; drills                   | Learners and schools reached                             |\n| Local radio and social media                            | Seasonal reminders; explainers on warnings                | Listenership or reach reported by the outlet or platform |\n| Printed and visual materials                            | Posters at evacuation centres, health centres and markets | Places posted and checked                                |\n| Community events (fiestas, market days)                 | Booths and demonstrations                                 | Visitors counted                                         |\n| Sector organisations (PWD, older persons, women, youth) | Messages designed with and for them                       | Members reached; feedback received                       |\n\n### 6.2 Local Language and Accessible Formats\n\nMessages are written in Ormocanon (Bisaya), with English where needed, and tested with residents before use (A6.4). Each core message has accessible versions: Filipino Sign Language video (RA 11106), large-print and pictorial versions, plain-language audio, and visual alerts for Deaf and hard-of-hearing residents. Where a message relays an official PAGASA or PHIVOLCS product, the translation keeps its meaning and the original wording is kept on file (Chapter 4).\n\n### 6.3 Risk Communication Plan and Public Information Officer\n\nThe City designates a DRRM public information officer and an alternate, and adopts a risk communication plan (A6.5) that sets spokespersons, approval of messages, coordination with the City Information Office, media relations, monitoring of rumours and misinformation, and correction. Between events the plan governs the IEC programme; during events Chapter 4 governs what is issued and by whom.\n\n### 6.4 Preparing the Public for Anticipatory Action\n\nRA 12287 adds a new message: the City may declare a State of Imminent Disaster and ask people to act days before impact. The IEC programme explains this before any declaration, so that the public notices issued under Chapter 4 (A6.7) are understood when they come. Geographically isolated barangays are reached through their BDRRMCs and VHF radio, not only by mobile networks.\n\n## 7. The City DRRM Training Programme\n\n### 7.1 Needs Analysis and Annual Plan\n\nEach year the CDRRMO, with the HRMO, analyses training needs across City offices, the EOC roster, BDRRMCs and BHERTs, schools, responders and volunteers, using the competency matrix (Annex 9-B), the skills inventory, IAT gaps and AAR findings (SOP 9-01). The analysis for CDRRMO staff is done under Chapter 2 (HR-04) and folded in. The result is one annual City DRRM training plan with courses, target groups, providers (CDRRMO trainers, OCD VIII, DOH, DSWD, BFP, Red Cross, academic partners), schedule, cost and evaluation method, funded through the LDRRMFIP (Chapter 6).\n\n### 7.2 Delivery, Records and Evidence of Competence\n\nTraining records state the course, provider, dates, participant (by position and office, or barangay), competency addressed, assessment result and refresher date (CAP-F03). Records of City employees are also sent to the HRMO. Competence is shown by assessment at the end of the course and by performance in an exercise or event. MODE does not yet hold training records; until it does, CAP-F03 is kept as a controlled register.\n\n### 7.3 Refresher Cycle\n\n**Table 9.5**\n\n*Proposed Refresher Intervals*\n\n| **Competency or Role**                                             | **Interval**                                                       | **Basis**                        |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| Basic life support and standard first aid                          | As the certifying body requires                                    | Certificate validity             |\n| Water search and rescue, collapsed structure and high-angle rescue | Annual practical refresher                                         | Safety-critical skills           |\n| ICS and EOC position training (EOC roster)                         | Annual refresher; exercise each year                               | B7.2, B7.3                       |\n| BDRRMC orientation                                                 | After each barangay election; annual refresher                     | A7.8; turnover                   |\n| BHERT training                                                     | Annual                                                             | Ordinance No. 171, s. 2024       |\n| Accredited volunteer training                                      | Annual                                                             | Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024; A2.8 |\n| RDANA, PDNA, MDM and CCCM teams                                    | Every two years and before the typhoon season for designated teams | Chapters 4 and 5                 |\n\n*Note. Intervals are proposals of this chapter for CDRRMC adoption, except where a certifying body sets its own. Attendance and lapse are tracked on CAP-F03 (A7.8).*\n\n### 7.4 Skills Inventory\n\nEvery 12 months the CDRRMO updates an inventory of DRRM-relevant skills across the CDRRMO, CDRRMC member offices, BDRRMCs, BHERTs and accredited volunteers: search and rescue, first aid, engineering, logistics, GIS, damage assessment, PDNA, MHPSS, communications, and languages including Filipino Sign Language (A2.10). It records the number of people with each skill by office or barangay and the date of their last training. It feeds the needs analysis and the EOC''s surge planning (Chapter 2).\n\n## 8. Drills and Exercises\n\nThe City exercises its plans in a progression (Figure 9.3). Drills test one task (evacuation of a school, a siren test, a call-down). Tabletop exercises test plans and decisions around a scenario. Functional exercises activate the EOC against a simulated event without deploying field teams. Full-scale exercises bring the EOC, field teams and partners together. Each is planned with objectives, evaluated under Chapter 7 (SOP 7-04) and followed by tracked actions.\n\n**Figure 9.3**\n\n*Progressive Exercise Program*\n\n![Figure 98: Progressive Exercise Program](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-98.webp)\n\n*Note. Prepared for this chapter. Exercise types follow common emergency-management practice; the EOC Capability Assessment Checklist and NDRRMC NSED guidelines apply where relevant.*\n\n**Table 9.6**\n\n*Minimum Annual Exercise Program*\n\n| **Exercise**                                                                                              | **Frequency**                              | **Participants and Focus**                                                               |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Nationwide Simultaneous Earthquake Drill                                                                  | Quarterly, per NDRRMC schedule             | City offices, schools, barangays; evacuation and headcount; evaluation filed (A7.2)      |\n| Hazard-specific drill (flash flood in the Anilao–Malbasag area, landslide, tsunami for coastal barangays) | At least one per priority hazard each year | Barangays at risk; warning to action; 12-hour welfare check (A7.9)                       |\n| Contingency plan tabletop exercise                                                                        | At least one plan each year                | CDRRMC member offices, schools, hospitals, utilities (A7.7)                              |\n| EOC functional exercise                                                                                   | Annual                                     | EOC roster; activation, SitRep cycle, resource requests, alternate EOC relocation (B7.3) |\n| Anticipatory action tabletop exercise                                                                     | Annual, before the typhoon season          | PDRA to declaration request, AA fund release (Chapter 6), public notice (Chapter 4)      |\n| Full-scale exercise                                                                                       | Every two years                            | EOC, IMT, field teams, partners and a neighbouring LGU under mutual aid (Chapter 8)      |\n\n*Note. Frequencies other than the NSED are proposals for CDRRMC adoption.*\n\n## 9. Barangay and Community Resilience\n\n### 9.1 BDRRMC Functionality and Support\n\nEvery year the CDRRMO, with the DILG City Office, assesses the functionality of all 85 BDRRMCs (A7.3), using the DILG barangay preparedness assessment (Listo si KAP, as cited in IAT v1.7) and the City''s functionality matrix (CAP-F06): organisation, plan reviewed (Chapter 3), early warning, evacuation, BHERT, equipment, drills held and reporting. Barangays are grouped into three support tiers (Figure 9.4). Low-functioning barangays and those with the highest exposure in the CDRA receive a priority package; higher-functioning barangays become peer mentors.\n\n**Figure 9.4**\n\n*Tiered Support for Barangay DRRM Committees*\n\n![Figure 99: Tiered Support for Barangay DRRM Committees](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-99.webp)\n\n*Note. Prepared for this chapter. Tier thresholds are set by the CDRRMC from the first assessment; none is fixed here.*\n\n### 9.2 Accounting for Residents Within 12 Hours\n\nAfter an event, each affected barangay should be able to say, within 12 hours of impact, how many of its residents are safe, injured, missing or displaced, purok by purok (A7.9). The BDRRMC does this through purok leaders using the household lists the barangay already keeps, and reports counts, never names, to the EOC. The welfare check is practised in the hazard-specific drill and timed (CAP-F07). Named lists stay with the barangay and the CSWDO (Chapter 7, SOP 7-09).\n\n### 9.3 Barangay Health Emergency Response Teams\n\nOrdinance No. 171, s. 2024 provides for BHERTs in all barangays. Their training and refreshers are part of the City training plan, with the City Health Office, and their readiness is part of the BDRRMC functionality assessment.\n\n## 10. Volunteers\n\nThe City volunteer programme (Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024) and the accreditation of community disaster volunteers (Res. No. 07, s. 2024) give the City a reserve of trained people. Chapter 2 (section 14.3) sets the HR rules: volunteers supplement and never replace required positions, and their roles, supervision, safety, insurance and limits are documented. This chapter runs the programme (SOP 9-07): recruitment, accreditation, registry, training, insurance under the risk categorisation adopted in 2022, and deployment rules. During activation, volunteers are tasked through the EOC or the Incident Commander, not directly by the organisations that recruited them. Accredited volunteers may be given anticipatory action tasks under RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 5, such as helping with pre-emptive evacuation and checking on at-risk households. Spontaneous volunteers in an event are registered at a single point and tasked, or politely redirected.\n\n## 11. Evacuation Centres, Schools, Hospitals and Inclusion\n\n### 11.1 Evacuation Centre Standards\n\nEvacuation centres are audited against RA 12076, DSWD standards and the NDRRMC Disaster Preparedness Minimum Standards (SOP 9-08; Annex 9-D): location in a low-risk area; structural safety (the Building Official''s assessment under Chapter 8, C2.1); WASH facilities separated by sex; privacy and family spaces; ground-floor priority and access for persons with disabilities, older persons and pregnant women; child-friendly spaces; a health and first-aid area; security; kitchen and storage; and power and communications. Retrofits are prioritised for centres serving the 46 critical flood barangays and those rated "Poor" in the CDRA, and funded through the LDRRMFIP (Chapter 6). Schools are used as evacuation centres only as a last resort and for the shortest time, consistent with RA 10821.\n\n### 11.2 Schools and Hospitals\n\nDepEd SDO schools, the tertiary institutions in the Education Resilience and DRRM Coordination Group, and the City''s hospitals and health facilities take part in contingency plan tabletop exercises and in drills each year (A7.7). Their own preparedness plans remain theirs; the CDRRMO aligns triggers (class suspension under Ordinance Nos. 135, s. 2023 and 189, s. 2025; hospital surge) and communications with them. Education and health anticipatory actions are Part C items owned by DepEd and the CHO (Chapter 8).\n\n### 11.3 Inclusive DRRM\n\nWomen, children, older persons, persons with disabilities, indigenous peoples and other at-risk groups take part in designing messages, trainings, drills and evacuation centre improvements, through the IPGDO, the PDAO, the OSCA and their organisations. Once a year, a short consultation with sector representatives asks whether they were engaged and what should change, and their answers are recorded as evidence for A7.6 (CAP-F05). Data on participation and reach are disaggregated by sex, age and disability where this can be done without identifying anyone.\n\n## 12. MODE Support\n\nMODE holds the EOC roster and designations (Chapter 2), the protocols run in drills (each run is a record), barangay reports and resource requests, and the Library and Vault where training materials and exercise reports are kept. It does not yet hold training records, the skills inventory, the BDRRMC functionality matrix or the volunteer registry. Until it does, these are kept on CAP forms as controlled registers; section 16 lists the platform additions. Any future table holding volunteer or trainee personal data follows Chapter 7 (SOP 7-09): positions and counts in reports, names only where the City as employer or programme owner needs them, with access limited by role.\n\n## 13. Standard Operating Procedures\n\nEach SOP has a control block and a step table. Every step states the actor (a position or office, never a person), the action and decision rule, inputs and outputs, and timing, control and escalation. Form codes are those of section 17. All MODE templates named here are proposals; none of SOPs 9-01 to 9-09 is yet loaded in MODE.\n\n### SOP 9-01 \\| Training Needs Analysis, Skills Inventory and Annual City DRRM Training Plan\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Administration and Training, with the City HRMO. **Purpose:** Base the City''s DRRM training on measured needs across offices, barangays, schools, responders and volunteers, and know each year what skills the City has.\n\n**Table 9.7**\n\n**SOP 9-01 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                                      |\n|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-01 (proposed)                                                                                |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMO Head approves the plan; CDRRMC endorses; funding through the LDRRMFIP (Chapter 6)                       |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Administration and Training                                                                             |\n| Trigger                     | Annual planning cycle (before the budget call); after a barangay election; after an AAR with training findings |\n| Forms                       | CAP-F01; CAP-F02; Annex 9-B                                                                                    |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A7.1 (2, Medium); A2.10 (1, Low); A2.6 (1, Medium, with Chapter 2)                                             |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-04 (A7.1); MR-02, MR-18 (A2.10)                                                                             |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10121 Secs. 12(c), 14; CDRRMC Res. No. 09, s. 2024                                                          |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 2 (HR-04); Chapter 6 (SOP 6-01); Chapter 7 (SOP 7-04)                                                  |\n\n**Table 9.8**\n\n**SOP 9-01 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Update the skills inventory</th>\n<th>Administration and Training with offices and BDRRMCs</th>\n<th><p>Collect counts of people with each DRRM skill by office and barangay, with last training date.</p>\n<p>Decision: All offices and barangays reported? Blank means "not reported".</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Templates; HRMO records</p>\n<p>Out: CAP-F01 inventory</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By March</p>\n<p>Control: Reporting rate recorded</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Analyse needs</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Compare the competency matrix (Annex 9-B) with the inventory, IAT gaps, AAR findings and lapsed refreshers; include CDRRMO staff needs from HR-04.</p>\n<p>Decision: Each gap traced to a role and a source?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F01; IAT; AARs; CAP-F03</p>\n<p>Out: Needs analysis</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By April</p>\n<p>Control: Life-safety and legal gaps first</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Draft the plan</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>List courses, target groups, providers, dates, cost and evaluation method; include refreshers and post-election orientation.</p>\n<p>Decision: Every course has an evaluation method?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Needs analysis</p>\n<p>Out: CAP-F02 draft plan</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By May</p>\n<p>Control: Providers confirmed</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Approve and fund</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head; CDRRMC</th>\n<th><p>Approve; include the cost in the LDRRMFIP.</p>\n<p>Decision: Funded?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F02</p>\n<p>Out: Approved plan; LDRRMFIP line</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: With the budget call</p>\n<p>Control: Line visible in FIN-F02</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Publish</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Publish the calendar to offices, barangays and partners.</p>\n<p>Decision: Sent to all target groups?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Approved plan</p>\n<p>Out: Training calendar</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Before the year starts</p>\n<p>Control: Filed in the Vault</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** CAP-F01 skills inventory; needs analysis; CAP-F02 annual plan; LDRRMFIP line; published calendar.\n\n### SOP 9-02 \\| Training Delivery, Competence Records and Refresher Cycle\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Administration and Training. **Purpose:** Deliver training that produces competence, keep verifiable records, and refresh skills before they lapse.\n\n**Table 9.9**\n\n**SOP 9-02 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                       |\n|-----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-02 (proposed)                                                 |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMO Head; HRMO for City employees'' records                                   |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Administration and Training                                              |\n| Trigger                     | Each scheduled course; a refresher date reached; a new BDRRMC after an election |\n| Forms                       | CAP-F03                                                                         |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A7.1 (2, Medium); A7.8 (1, Low); B7.2 (2, Medium)                               |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-04; MR-18                                                                    |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10121 Sec. 14; NDRRMC MC No. 4, s. 2012                                      |\n| Cross-references            | SOP 9-01; Chapter 2 (HR-04)                                                     |\n\n**Table 9.10**\n\n**SOP 9-02 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Prepare</th>\n<th>Course lead</th>\n<th><p>Confirm objectives, competencies, trainers, venue, materials (local language and accessible), and participant list by position.</p>\n<p>Decision: Accessible formats ready?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F02</p>\n<p>Out: Course file</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Two weeks before</p>\n<p>Control: Inclusion checklist</p>\n<p>Escalate: Administration and Training</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Deliver</th>\n<th>Trainers</th>\n<th><p>Run the course with practical exercises.</p>\n<p>Decision: Practical component included?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Course file</p>\n<p>Out: Attendance</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: As scheduled</p>\n<p>Control: Attendance signed</p>\n<p>Escalate: Course lead</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Assess</th>\n<th>Trainers</th>\n<th><p>Assess each participant against the competency.</p>\n<p>Decision: Competent, or needs further training?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Assessment tools</p>\n<p>Out: Assessment results</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: End of course</p>\n<p>Control: Results recorded, not only attendance</p>\n<p>Escalate: Course lead</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Record</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Enter course, participant (position, office or barangay), competency, result and refresher date on CAP-F03; send City employees'' records to the HRMO.</p>\n<p>Decision: Refresher date set?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Results</p>\n<p>Out: CAP-F03 entries</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 10 days</p>\n<p>Control: Second person checks entries</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Track refreshers</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Each quarter, list people whose refresher is due within 90 days or lapsed; schedule them.</p>\n<p>Decision: Lapsed refreshers in safety-critical roles? Remove from active deployment lists until refreshed.</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F03</p>\n<p>Out: Refresher list</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Quarterly</p>\n<p>Control: List sent to supervisors</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Evaluate</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Check whether trained people performed in exercises and events (Chapter 7 AARs).</p>\n<p>Decision: Training effective?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: AARs; exercise reports</p>\n<p>Out: Course evaluation</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Changes to the next plan</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Course files; attendance; assessment results; CAP-F03; refresher lists; course evaluations.\n\n### SOP 9-03 \\| Annual Exercise Programme: Planning, Conduct and Follow-Up\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Administration and Training, with an exercise director named for each exercise. **Purpose:** Test plans, the EOC and people in a planned progression every year, and turn the findings into improvements.\n\n**Table 9.11**\n\n**SOP 9-03 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                        |\n|-----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-03 (proposed); MODE protocol runs in drill mode                                |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMC approves the annual programme; CDRRMO Head approves each exercise plan                    |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Administration and Training                                                               |\n| Trigger                     | Annual planning; NSED schedule; new or revised contingency plan; AAR finding                     |\n| Forms                       | CAP-F04; ME-F04 (evaluation, Chapter 7)                                                          |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A7.2 (2, Medium); B7.3 (2, Medium); A7.7 (3, Low); A7.9 (1, Medium)                              |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-04; MR-11; MR-18                                                                              |\n| Policy basis                | NDRRMC NSED guidelines; EOC Capability Assessment Checklist; RA 10121                            |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 3 (contingency plans); Chapter 4 (protocols); Chapter 7 (SOP 7-04); Chapter 8 (partners) |\n\n**Table 9.12**\n\n**SOP 9-03 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Plan the year</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Set the programme from Table 9.6: which plans, hazards, barangays and partners each exercise covers.</p>\n<p>Decision: Each priority hazard and the EOC covered?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CPs; IAT; AARs</p>\n<p>Out: Annual exercise programme</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: With the training plan</p>\n<p>Control: CDRRMC approval</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Design the exercise</th>\n<th>Exercise director</th>\n<th><p>Write objectives, scenario, participants, safety rules, evaluator list and exercise control (CAP-F04).</p>\n<p>Decision: Objectives measurable?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Program</p>\n<p>Out: CAP-F04 exercise plan</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Six weeks before (two for drills)</p>\n<p>Control: CDRRMO Head approves</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Prepare</th>\n<th>Exercise director</th>\n<th><p>Brief players and evaluators; inform the public and partners where needed; prepare MODE protocols in drill mode.</p>\n<p>Decision: Public informed where the exercise is visible?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F04</p>\n<p>Out: Briefings; notices</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: One week before</p>\n<p>Control: "Exercise" marked on all messages</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Conduct</th>\n<th>Exercise director; controllers</th>\n<th><p>Run the exercise; stop it if real safety is at risk or a real event occurs.</p>\n<p>Decision: Real event? Stop and switch to operations.</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Exercise plan</p>\n<p>Out: Logs; protocol runs</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: As scheduled</p>\n<p>Control: Safety officer present</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Hot wash</th>\n<th>Exercise director</th>\n<th><p>Collect immediate observations from players and evaluators.</p>\n<p>Decision: All teams heard?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Logs</p>\n<p>Out: Hot-wash notes</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: End of exercise</p>\n<p>Control: Notes filed</p>\n<p>Escalate: —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Evaluate</th>\n<th>Evaluators; M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Evaluate against objectives under Chapter 7 (SOP 7-04); time the welfare check where included.</p>\n<p>Decision: Findings with evidence?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Notes; logs</p>\n<p>Out: ME-F04 report; CAP-F07 times</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within the Chapter 7 timing</p>\n<p>Control: CDRRMO Head approves</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Follow up</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Enter actions in the CAPA register; feed the training plan.</p>\n<p>Decision: Every finding has an action?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: ME-F04</p>\n<p>Out: ME-F05 entries</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 5 days of approval</p>\n<p>Control: Tracked to closure</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Annual programme; CAP-F04 plans; briefings and notices; logs and protocol runs; hot-wash notes; ME-F04 evaluations; CAPA entries.\n\n### SOP 9-04 \\| Public Awareness (IEC) Programme With Reach Targets\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Administration and Training, with the City Information Office. **Purpose:** Reach every barangay each year with clear, accessible preparedness messages, and measure the reach.\n\n**Table 9.13**\n\n**SOP 9-04 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                           |\n|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-04 (proposed)                                                                     |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMO Head approves the programme; the Mayor or designated spokesperson approves public statements |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Administration and Training                                                                  |\n| Trigger                     | Annual planning; start of the typhoon season; NSED; new hazard information                          |\n| Forms                       | CAP-F05                                                                                             |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A6.6 (2, Low); A6.4 (2, Medium); A7.6 (3, Low)                                                      |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-06 (A6.4, A6.6); MR-04, MR-09, MR-16, MR-18 (A7.6)                                               |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10121 Sec. 12(c); RA 11106; RA 7277 as amended; RA 10639                                         |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 3 (hazard maps); Chapter 4 (warnings); SOP 9-05                                             |\n\n**Table 9.14**\n\n**SOP 9-04 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Set targets</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Set messages, audiences, channels and reach targets per barangay; prioritise the most exposed barangays.</p>\n<p>Decision: Targets per barangay set?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CDRA; last year''s reach</p>\n<p>Out: CAP-F05 programme</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By February</p>\n<p>Control: CDRRMO Head approves</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Develop materials</th>\n<th>Administration and Training with sector groups</th>\n<th><p>Write in Ormocanon (Bisaya); produce FSL, large-print, pictorial and audio versions; test with residents.</p>\n<p>Decision: Understood by test groups?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Messages</p>\n<p>Out: Tested materials</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By March</p>\n<p>Control: Sector groups consulted</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Deliver</th>\n<th>Administration and Training; BDRRMCs; schools; partners</th>\n<th><p>Run activities by channel; count reach by barangay, sex and age group where possible.</p>\n<p>Decision: On track by mid-year?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Materials</p>\n<p>Out: Reach log</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Through the year</p>\n<p>Control: Counts, not names</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Check knowledge</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Run a short knowledge check in a sample of barangays.</p>\n<p>Decision: Key messages understood?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Sample</p>\n<p>Out: Knowledge check results</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Method recorded</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Report</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Report reach against targets in the annual results report (Chapter 7).</p>\n<p>Decision: Targets met? If not, why?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Reach log; results</p>\n<p>Out: IEC report</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: December</p>\n<p>Control: Included in ME-F02</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** CAP-F05 programme; tested materials; reach log; knowledge check results; IEC report.\n\n### SOP 9-05 \\| Risk Communication Plan and Public Information Officer\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with the City Information Office. **Purpose:** Have a designated PIO and an approved plan for consistent, accurate and inclusive public communication, including rumour management.\n\n**Table 9.15**\n\n**SOP 9-05 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                            |\n|-----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-05 (proposed)                                                      |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | City Mayor designates the PIO and approves the plan                                  |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Head                                                                          |\n| Trigger                     | Adoption of this chapter; annual review; change of PIO; AAR finding on communication |\n| Forms                       | Risk communication plan (Annex to this chapter, to be prepared)                      |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A6.5 (2, Low); A6.4 (2, Medium)                                                      |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-06                                                                                |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10121; RA 11106; RA 10173                                                         |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 4 (event-time communication); SOP 9-04                                       |\n\n**Table 9.16**\n\n**SOP 9-05 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Designate</th>\n<th>City Mayor</th>\n<th><p>Designate the DRRM PIO and an alternate.</p>\n<p>Decision: Designated in writing?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Nomination</p>\n<p>Out: Office order</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 30 days of adoption</p>\n<p>Control: Filed in the Vault</p>\n<p>Escalate: —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Draft the plan</th>\n<th>PIO with City Information Office</th>\n<th><p>Set spokespersons, message approval, media relations, channels, accessibility, rumour monitoring and correction, and coordination with Chapter 4.</p>\n<p>Decision: Consistent with Chapter 4?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Chapters 4 and 9</p>\n<p>Out: Draft plan</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 60 days</p>\n<p>Control: Legal review where needed</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Approve</th>\n<th>City Mayor</th>\n<th><p>Approve the plan.</p>\n<p>Decision: Approved?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Draft</p>\n<p>Out: Approved plan</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 90 days</p>\n<p>Control: Version recorded</p>\n<p>Escalate: —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Practise</th>\n<th>PIO</th>\n<th><p>Use the plan in exercises (SOP 9-03), including a misinformation scenario.</p>\n<p>Decision: Worked under exercise conditions?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Exercise plan</p>\n<p>Out: Exercise findings</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Findings to the CAPA register</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Review</th>\n<th>PIO</th>\n<th><p>Review the plan each year and after events.</p>\n<p>Decision: Changes needed?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: AARs</p>\n<p>Out: Revised plan</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Revision record kept</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Designation order; risk communication plan and versions; exercise findings.\n\n### SOP 9-06 \\| BDRRMC Functionality Assessment and Tiered Support\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning, with the DILG City Office and the Liga ng mga Barangay. **Purpose:** Know each year how well each of the 85 BDRRMCs functions and support the weakest first.\n\n**Table 9.17**\n\n**SOP 9-06 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                       |\n|-----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-06 (proposed)                                                 |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMC approves the support plan; barangays decide on their own BDRRMC actions  |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                    |\n| Trigger                     | Annual cycle; after a barangay election; after an event that exposed a weakness |\n| Forms                       | CAP-F06; CAP-F07                                                                |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A7.3 (2, Medium); A7.9 (1, Medium); A3.7 (3, Low, Chapter 3)                    |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-04; MR-18                                                                    |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10121; DILG MC No. 2025-035 (Listo si KAP); Ordinance No. 171, s. 2024       |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 3 (BDRRMP review, EO 2023-0085); SOPs 9-02, 9-03                        |\n\n**Table 9.18**\n\n**SOP 9-06 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Assess</th>\n<th>Research and Planning with DILG City Office</th>\n<th><p>Assess all 85 BDRRMCs on CAP-F06, using Listo si KAP results where available.</p>\n<p>Decision: All 85 assessed? Blank means "not assessed".</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Barangay records; visits</p>\n<p>Out: CAP-F06 matrix</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By March</p>\n<p>Control: Evidence noted per item</p>\n<p>Escalate: DILG City Director</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Tier</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Group barangays into support tiers using thresholds set by the CDRRMC and CDRA exposure.</p>\n<p>Decision: Most exposed low-functioning barangays first?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F06; CDRA</p>\n<p>Out: Tier list</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By April</p>\n<p>Control: Thresholds documented</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Plan support</th>\n<th>Research and Planning with Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Agree a support package per tier: training, plan review, drill, equipment check, mentoring by higher-tier barangays.</p>\n<p>Decision: Resources available?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Tier list</p>\n<p>Out: Support plan</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By May</p>\n<p>Control: CDRRMC approves</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Deliver</th>\n<th>CDRRMO teams; peer mentors</th>\n<th><p>Deliver support; include a timed purok welfare check in each barangay''s drill.</p>\n<p>Decision: Welfare check within 12 hours?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Support plan</p>\n<p>Out: Visit records; CAP-F07 times</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Through the year</p>\n<p>Control: Counts only reported</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Re-assess and report</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Re-assess next year; report change by tier to the CDRRMC.</p>\n<p>Decision: Did the weakest improve?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F06</p>\n<p>Out: Report</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Included in ME-F02</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** CAP-F06 matrix; tier list; support plan; visit records; CAP-F07 welfare-check times; annual report.\n\n### SOP 9-07 \\| Volunteer Programme: Recruitment, Accreditation, Registry, Training, Insurance and Deployment\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Administration and Training. **Purpose:** Keep a trained, insured and accredited volunteer reserve that can be tasked safely through the EOC.\n\n**Table 9.19**\n\n**SOP 9-07 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                                                               |\n|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-07 (proposed)                                                                                                         |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMC accredits volunteers (RA 10121 Sec. 13); EOC or Incident Commander tasks during activation                                       |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Administration and Training                                                                                                      |\n| Trigger                     | Annual recruitment; an organisation''s request for accreditation; an activation; a spontaneous volunteer influx                          |\n| Forms                       | CAP-F08                                                                                                                                 |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A2.8 (3, Low)                                                                                                                           |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-02; MR-16                                                                                                                            |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10121 Sec. 13; RA 9418; Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024; CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03–04, s. 2022 and No. 07, s. 2024; RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 5 |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 2 (section 14.3); Chapter 8 (CPR-RIMT MOA); Chapter 4 (tasking); Chapter 7 (SOP 7-09)                                           |\n\n**Table 9.20**\n\n**SOP 9-07 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Recruit</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Invite individuals and organisations; state roles, requirements and commitments.</p>\n<p>Decision: Eligible?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Program rules</p>\n<p>Out: Applications</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Minimum age and fitness rules applied</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Train and accredit</th>\n<th>Administration and Training; CDRRMC</th>\n<th><p>Train applicants; CDRRMC accredits those who pass.</p>\n<p>Decision: Competent?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Training results</p>\n<p>Out: Accreditation resolution</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Per cycle</p>\n<p>Control: Resolution filed</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Register and insure</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Enter accredited volunteers on CAP-F08 with skills, availability, insurance category and expiry.</p>\n<p>Decision: Insured before deployment?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Accreditation</p>\n<p>Out: CAP-F08 registry; insurance cover</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: On accreditation</p>\n<p>Control: Personal data access limited by role</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Deploy</th>\n<th>EOC or Incident Commander</th>\n<th><p>Task volunteers through the EOC or IC; brief on safety; record check-in and check-out.</p>\n<p>Decision: Task within training and insurance?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F08</p>\n<p>Out: Deployment records</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: During activation or AA</p>\n<p>Control: No deployment without briefing</p>\n<p>Escalate: EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Manage spontaneous volunteers</th>\n<th>EOC Logistics</th>\n<th><p>Register at one point; task to safe roles or redirect.</p>\n<p>Decision: Safe and useful task available?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Registration</p>\n<p>Out: Spontaneous volunteer log</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: During events</p>\n<p>Control: No unregistered deployment</p>\n<p>Escalate: EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Refresh and review</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Refresh training yearly; renew insurance; review the registry.</p>\n<p>Decision: Lapsed? Mark inactive.</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F08</p>\n<p>Out: Updated registry</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Inactive volunteers not tasked</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Applications; training results; accreditation resolutions; CAP-F08 registry; insurance records; deployment logs.\n\n### SOP 9-08 \\| Evacuation Centre Standards Audit and Improvement\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning, with the CSWDO, the Building Official and barangays. **Purpose:** Know whether each evacuation centre meets the standards and improve them in priority order.\n\n**Table 9.21**\n\n**SOP 9-08 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                                     |\n|-----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-08 (proposed)                                                                               |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | Building Official for structural safety; CDRRMC prioritises; SP appropriates retrofit funds                   |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                                                  |\n| Trigger                     | Annual cycle; a new or changed evacuation centre; an AAR finding on a centre                                  |\n| Forms                       | CAP-F09; Annex 9-D                                                                                            |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A7.4 (2, High); C2.1 (1, High, Chapter 8)                                                                     |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-04 (A7.4); MR-03, MR-16, MR-17, MR-18 (C2.1)                                                               |\n| Policy basis                | RA 12076; DSWD standards; NDRRMC Disaster Preparedness Minimum Standards Vol. 2; RA 10821; RA 7277 as amended |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 4 (evacuation); Chapter 6 (funding); Chapter 8 (C2.1)                                                 |\n\n**Table 9.22**\n\n**SOP 9-08 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. List centres</th>\n<th>Research and Planning with CSWDO</th>\n<th><p>List all designated evacuation centres with capacity, barangays served and owner.</p>\n<p>Decision: List matches Chapter 4 plans?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CPs; CSWDO list</p>\n<p>Out: EC register</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By February</p>\n<p>Control: One list in the Vault</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Audit</th>\n<th>Audit team (CDRRMO, CSWDO, barangay)</th>\n<th><p>Check each centre against Annex 9-D; request the Building Official''s structural assessment.</p>\n<p>Decision: Each item checked with evidence?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Annex 9-D</p>\n<p>Out: CAP-F09 audit</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: March–April</p>\n<p>Control: Photos filed; no personal data</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Prioritise</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Rank gaps by life-safety, exposure (46 critical flood barangays; CDRA "Poor" ratings) and number served.</p>\n<p>Decision: Ranking agreed?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: CAP-F09; CDRA</p>\n<p>Out: Priority list</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By May</p>\n<p>Control: CDRRMC approves</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Fund and improve</th>\n<th>CDRRMO with City Engineer and CSWDO</th>\n<th><p>Put retrofits in the LDRRMFIP; deliver works and supplies.</p>\n<p>Decision: Funded?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Priority list</p>\n<p>Out: LDRRMFIP lines; completed works</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Per budget cycle</p>\n<p>Control: Chapter 6 rules</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Re-audit</th>\n<th>Audit team</th>\n<th><p>Re-audit improved centres and report.</p>\n<p>Decision: Standards met?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Completed works</p>\n<p>Out: Updated CAP-F09</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Reported in ME-F02</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** EC register; CAP-F09 audits; structural assessments; priority list; LDRRMFIP lines; re-audits.\n\n### SOP 9-09 \\| Sector Consultation on Inclusive DRRM\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with the IPGDO, PDAO and OSCA. **Purpose:** Hear each year from women, children and youth, older persons, persons with disabilities and other at-risk groups whether they were engaged, and act on what they say.\n\n**Table 9.23**\n\n**SOP 9-09 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                               |\n|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP9-09 (proposed)                                                         |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMO Head; CDRRMC for actions beyond the CDRRMO                                       |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Head                                                                             |\n| Trigger                     | Annual cycle, before the IAT cycle; after a major event                                 |\n| Forms                       | CAP-F05 (consultation part)                                                             |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A7.6 (3, Low); A6.4 (2, Medium)                                                         |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-04; MR-09; MR-16; MR-18 (A7.6); MR-06 (A6.4)                                         |\n| Policy basis                | RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 9994; RA 10821; RA 8371; RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 1(a) |\n| Cross-references            | SOPs 9-04, 9-08; Chapter 7 (SOP 7-08)                                                   |\n\n**Table 9.24**\n\n**SOP 9-09 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Invite</th>\n<th>CDRRMO with IPGDO, PDAO, OSCA</th>\n<th><p>Invite sector representatives; arrange accessible venue, interpreters and materials.</p>\n<p>Decision: Each sector represented?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Sector lists</p>\n<p>Out: Invitations</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Accessibility checklist</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Consult</th>\n<th>Facilitator</th>\n<th><p>Ask whether they were engaged in plans, trainings, drills and EC improvements, and what should change.</p>\n<p>Decision: All voices heard?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Prepared questions</p>\n<p>Out: Consultation record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Session</p>\n<p>Control: Record by sector, not by name</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Act</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Decide on changes; enter them in the CAPA register; report back to participants.</p>\n<p>Decision: Changes assigned?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Record</p>\n<p>Out: ME-F05 entries; feedback to participants</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 30 days</p>\n<p>Control: Feedback sent</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Invitations; consultation records; CAPA entries; feedback to participants.\n\n## 14. Responsibility Assignment (RACI)\n\n| **\\[R\\] Responsible** | **\\[A\\] Accountable** | **\\[C\\] Consulted** | **\\[I\\] Informed** |\n|-----------------------|-----------------------|---------------------|--------------------|\n\n"A" is the single proposed process-accountable owner; "R" performs; "C" is consulted; "I" is informed. The RACI allocates work; it does not transfer legal powers.\n\n**Table 9.25**\n\n*RACI for Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development*\n\n| **Activity**                            | **MY** | **CC** | **HD** | **AT** | **RP** | **EOC** | **HR** | **SW** | **DI** | **BR** | **SC** |\n|-----------------------------------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|---------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n| TNA, skills inventory and training plan | I      | C      | A      | R      | C      | C       | R      | C      | C      | R      | C      |\n| Training delivery and refreshers        | –      | I      | A      | R      | C      | C       | R      | C      | I      | R      | C      |\n| Annual exercise programme               | I      | A      | R      | R      | C      | R       | –      | C      | C      | R      | C      |\n| IEC programme                           | I      | I      | A      | R      | C      | –       | –      | C      | C      | R      | R      |\n| Risk communication plan and PIO         | A      | I      | R      | C      | –      | C       | –      | –      | –      | –      | C      |\n| BDRRMC functionality and support        | I      | C      | A      | R      | R      | –       | –      | C      | R      | R      | C      |\n| Volunteer programme                     | I      | A      | R      | R      | –      | R       | C      | C      | –      | C      | –      |\n| Evacuation centre audit and improvement | I      | A      | R      | C      | R      | –       | –      | R      | –      | R      | C      |\n| Sector consultation on inclusion        | I      | I      | A      | R      | C      | –       | –      | C      | –      | C      | R      |\n\n*Note. MY = City Mayor; CC = CDRRMC; HD = CDRRMO Head; AT = CDRRMO Administration and Training; RP = CDRRMO Research and Planning; EOC = EOC Manager and staff; HR = City HRMO; SW = CSWDO; DI = DILG City Office; BR = barangays and BDRRMCs; SC = sector organisations (through the IPGDO, PDAO and OSCA). For the volunteer programme, the CDRRMC accredits; for evacuation centres, the CDRRMC sets priorities and the Building Official decides structural safety.*\n\n## 15. Performance Monitoring, Quality Assurance and Corrective Action\n\n**Table 9.26**\n\n*Performance Measures for Chapter 9*\n\n| **Measure**                                                                         | **Evidence**                | **Review Owner** |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|------------------|\n| Annual training plan approved and delivered as planned                              | CAP-F02; CAP-F03            | CDRRMC           |\n| Share of trained people assessed competent; share with refresher not lapsed         | CAP-F03                     | CDRRMO Head      |\n| Skills inventory updated within 12 months; share of offices and barangays reporting | CAP-F01                     | CDRRMO Head      |\n| Exercises held as programmed and evaluated; actions closed                          | CAP-F04; ME-F04; ME-F05     | CDRRMC           |\n| IEC reach against target per barangay; knowledge check results                      | CAP-F05                     | CDRRMC           |\n| BDRRMCs assessed; change in the low-functioning tier                                | CAP-F06                     | CDRRMC           |\n| Barangays completing a timed welfare check within 12 hours                          | CAP-F07                     | CDRRMO Head      |\n| Accredited, insured and active volunteers                                           | CAP-F08                     | CDRRMO Head      |\n| Evacuation centres meeting the audit standard                                       | CAP-F09                     | CDRRMC           |\n| Sector consultation held and actions taken                                          | Consultation record; ME-F05 | CDRRMO Head      |\n\nFindings go to the CAPA register under Chapter 7 (SOP 7-05).\n\n## 16. Implementation, Validation and Institutionalisation Priorities\n\n**Table 9.27**\n\n*Implementation Priorities for Chapter 9*\n\n','Loaded from reference/moo/consolidated/09_Chapter_9.md (split from MOO_Consolidated_v1.0.md, 12 Nov 2026)','draft')
